24523 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "5abea3ab-76a7-5598-8ede-b4cf88dd47d7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124571 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048505 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124571 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048669 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124571 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124571 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186125062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124571 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272092 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124571 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186272102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124571 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186323856 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124571 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186354507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124571 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366006 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124571 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124571 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186366511 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124571 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Eigenvalues of a 4x4 Matrix Containing a Block of Zeros" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124571 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186124380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124571 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "ef311af0-0e95-5574-a151-2971ad66bd08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124749 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124749 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124749 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124749 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124749 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186125488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124749 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124749 #edb/ref 1045 "The Characteristic Equation of a Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124749 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186125406 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124749 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124749 #edb/ref 1050 1080.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186124749 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "9ba6f288-55b6-582e-8aa1-d15ec442bd9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125062 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186323582 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125062 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37355" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125062 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125062 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -9 & -18 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -10 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -10 & 0 \\\\ -18 & 18 & -36 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n- $\\lambda =-10$ is an eigenvalue of $P$\n- The eigenvalue $\\lambda =-10$ has algebraic multiplicity $3$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125062 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c699a77c-6393-5572-b010-6743d9f3bb17" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125062 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we find the matrix $\\,P-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nP-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & -9 & -18 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -10-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -10-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -18 & 18 & -36 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(P-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nNotice that we can expand the $4\\times 4$ determinant across the $2$nd row, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & -9 & -18 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -10-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -10-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -18 & 18 & -36 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-10-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & -18 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -10-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -18 & -36 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3\\times 3$ determinant across the $2$nd row, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-10-\\lambda )\\cdot (-10-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -18 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-10-\\lambda )^{2}((-1-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )-54) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +10)^{2}({\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda -50) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +10)^{2}(\\lambda +10)(\\lambda -5) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +10)^{3}(\\lambda -5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $P$ are $\\lambda =-10$ (with algebraic multiplicity $3$) and $\\lambda =5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125406 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125407 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125406 #edb/ref 1061 20093 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125406 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9f05d37d-484a-5ae0-8ed9-0b46584f4a7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125406 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125407 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125407 #edb/ref 1061 20094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125407 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9edf1314-60a8-581e-afc3-f39ecd8b7951" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125407 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125408 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125408 #edb/ref 1061 20095 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125408 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "37883812-d28d-5bd5-97af-2df4b9f73f66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125408 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125409 #edb/ref 1061 20096 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125409 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d8240de1-8637-5d84-886a-269d69011445" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125409 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125488 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125409 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125488 #edb/ref 1061 25951 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125488 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1c9b6469-fe8d-5b75-bb95-efbf2684e9ce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125488 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125785 #edb/ref 1064 "We can find the eigenvalues of a $3\\times 3$ matrix in much the same way as for $2\\!\\times\\! 2$ matrices. \n\nRemember, $\\lambda$ is an eigenvalue of $A$ if $A\\mathbf{v} = \\lambda\\mathbf{v}$ for some non-zero vector $\\mathbf{v}$. This implies that the equation\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\mathbf{v}=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nhas a non-trivial solution, which means that the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, and consequently\n$\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0.$\nTherefore, if we want to find the eigenvalues of a $n\\!\\times\\! n$ matrix $A,$ we have to find the solutions of the characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0.\n$$\n\nLet's go through this process in the case of the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n![](images/21/21e0994519fbba32a84020ce4661e98600c1307947083ddc3afe9902658143c1.svg)\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix $A-\\lambda I{:}$\n\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\lambda \\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set and solve the characteristic equation $\\,\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ Using the fact that the determinant of a triangular (or diagonal) matrix is given by the product of the elements on the main diagonal, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )(-8-\\lambda )(5-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda +8)(\\lambda -5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ are $\\lambda=-2,$ $\\lambda=-8,$ and $\\lambda=5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125785 #edb/ref 1065 3564 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125785 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "535c5b8b-f815-5ded-8ae5-668bac420af7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125785 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125786 #edb/ref 1064 "The **algebraic multiplicity** of an eigenvalue $\\lambda$ is the number of times it is a solution of the characteristic equation. \n\nFor example, consider a $6\\times 6$ matrix $A$ with the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n(\\lambda-{\\color{red}4})(\\lambda-{\\color{red}4})(\\lambda-1)(\\lambda+8)^{\\color{blue}3} =0\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalue $\\lambda={\\color{red}4}$ is a double root. Therefore, it has algebraic multiplicity $2.$ \n\nBy the same reasoning, we say that \n\n- $\\lambda=1$ has algebraic multiplicity $1,$ and\n\n- $\\lambda=-8$ has algebraic multiplicity $\\color{blue}3$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125786 #edb/ref 1065 3565 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125786 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "db18f185-0dce-547f-95f2-71b60a6d4d2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125786 #edb/ref 1067 "The Algebraic Multiplicity of an Eigenvalue" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269747 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\lambda }^{3}-2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda -30=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269747 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269747 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "343afa09-d3de-5a19-916a-586f7a7d1e10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269929 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}+13\\lambda -15=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269929 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186269929 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "486477e7-c24f-5198-8b45-bd30ea74890e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270132 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\lambda }^{3}+13{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +4=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270132 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186270132 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6228aba2-8afa-597c-95c4-f06c42a62e0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271152 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\lambda }^{3}+2{\\lambda }^{2}-13\\lambda +10=0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271152 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186271152 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d6af2f78-c6df-57d2-ac96-1c55af1